# Untracked text files are ignored by extension, because the names are whatever wrote them: a
# scratch copy, an editor's export, a session's log. It is the one rule here whose reach is worth
# naming, because ignoring a file is also how a file is kept out of a commit without being kept out
# of the tree - and a file this rule hides is one neither path guard reads, since both read the set
# a commit would carry. `.agents/sample.machine.local.json.txt` is the case in hand: a machine file
# for the package next door, left in place by decision rather than adopted, held out of `git status`
# and out of both readings by this rule rather than by anything about the file. The rule stays; what
# it is holding is written down here so that whoever narrows it knows what it answers. An ignore
# rule that answers a guard is the quiet half of the failure AGENTS.md section 0 describes, and the
# fix is to say so rather than to let the silence read as a green.
*.txt
composer[0-9].json
/composer.phar
/hs_err_pid*.log
/opencode*.jsonc
/permissions-01.md
/oc-session-*.md

# The editor's agent writes this the first time a checkout is opened as a workspace, and what
# it holds is a project id: local to one machine and belonging to no version of this package.
/.freebuff/

# Machine-local settings. `.agents/machine.local.json` is the one file that names this machine's
# PHP, PowerShell and package directory; each settings file below is rendered from the committed
# `.example` template beside it by `python .agents/render_local.py`. Committing either one would
# put a drive path in the repository, which is what the two path guards exist to refuse.
/.agents/machine.local.json
/.trae/settings.json
/.trae/settings.local.json
/.kiro/settings/local.json

# The checks in `.agents/` are Python, and running one leaves the byte-code of the file it read
# beside that file. It is not a source and not a fact about a machine — it is the one artefact
# these checks produce themselves, and it is ignored rather than committed because the next run
# writes it again.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
